    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d456">

                <pb facs="0559" n="1393" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1393"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656.119/447a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Commissioner of
                        Docks and Ferries of New York City</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hulbert</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-28">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 28, 1918</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to your letter of the 21st
                        instant<anchor n="1" xml:id="d456-f1"/> you are advised that the Dutch ships
                    now lying idle in New York Harbor will very soon be freed, as the negotiations
                    which are being carried on by the Department of State and the War Trade Board
                    are now close to a successful termination. I think it is only a matter of a very
                    few days before all the vessels will be moving either to dry dock or to sea</p>
                <p>Thanking you for your interest in the matter,</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
